      "lem:finite-cell-asymptotics"
    ],
    "justification": "All effects share signed cell moments and denominators, so joint covariance propagation is necessary for simultaneous claims.",
    "gap": "Blackwell--Pashley provide the scalar and vector factorial-IV ingredients; this theorem records their adaptation to the Möbius vector and is an inference rung rather than the novelty kernel.",
    "consumer": "The result permits family-wise statements about all seven factorial effects in software and applied reanalysis when the perfect-complier share is not weak.",
    "proof_tex": "All assertions are pointwise at a fixed law satisfying the displayed assumptions.  Since \\(\\mathcal B\\) is finite and every \\(\\pi_z>0\\), the finite-cell asymptotic lemma applies jointly to all cell estimators.  Linear combination with the fixed coefficients \\(a_t(z)\\) yields\n\\[\n\\sqrt n(\\widehat p_t-p_t)\n=n^{-1/2}\\sum_{i=1}^n\\phi_{p,t}(O_i)+o_P(1),\n\\qquad\n\\sqrt n(\\widehat m_t-m_t)\n=n^{-1/2}\\sum_{i=1}^n\\phi_{m,t}(O_i)+o_P(1). \\tag{29}\n\\]\nBy signed identification, \\(p_t=p^\\star\\) and \\(m_t=p^\\star\\mu_t^\\star\\).  The lower bound \\(p^\\star\\ge\\kappa>0\\) and (29) imply \\(\\widehat p_t\\xrightarrow{P}p^\\star\\) and hence license the ratio expansion\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt n(\\widehat\\mu_t-\\mu_t^\\star)\n&=\\frac{1}{\\widehat p_t}\\sqrt n\n \\left[(\\widehat m_t-m_t)-\\mu_t^\\star(\\widehat p_t-p_t)\\right]\\\\\n&=n^{-1/2}\\sum_{i=1}^n\n \\frac{\\phi_{m,t}(O_i)-\\mu_t^\\star\\phi_{p,t}(O_i)}{p^\\star}\n +o_P(1)\\\\\n&=n^{-1/2}\\sum_{i=1}^n\\phi_{\\mu,t}(O_i)+o_P(1).\n\\end{aligned} \\tag{30}\n\\]\nStacking the eight equalities and left-multiplying by the fixed Walsh matrix \\(H\\) gives\n\\[\n\\sqrt n(\\widehat{\\boldsymbol\\theta}-\\boldsymbol\\theta)\n=n^{-1/2}\\sum_{i=1}^n\\phi_\\theta(O_i)+o_P(1). \\tag{31}\n\\]\nFinite second moments follow for all coordinates because assignment weights are finite constants, receipt indicators are bounded, \\(\\mathbb E_P[Y^2]<\\infty\\), and \\(p^\\star\\ge\\kappa\\).  The multivariate central limit conclusion in the cited lemma therefore gives\n\\[\nn^{-1/2}\\sum_{i=1}^n\\phi_\\theta(O_i)\\ \\Rightarrow\\ N(0,\\Omega). \\tag{32}\n\\]\n\nTo make the plug-in assertion explicit, take \\(\\widehat\\Omega\\) to be the empirical second moment of \\(\\phi_\\theta\\) after replacing every arm probability and cell moment by its empirical counterpart and replacing \\(p^\\star,\\mu_t^\\star\\) by \\(\\widehat p_t,\\widehat\\mu_t\\), with any fixed convention on the event that a denominator is zero.  Positive arm probabilities and \\(p^\\star\\ge\\kappa\\) make the probability of that exceptional event tend to zero.  The plug-in consistency part of the finite-cell lemma and continuous mapping yield\n\\[\n\\widehat\\Omega\\to_P\\Omega. \\tag{33}\n\\]\nPositive definiteness of \\(\\Omega\\) licenses inversion.  From (31)--(33) and the Wald conclusion of the cited lemma,\n\\[\nn(\\widehat{\\boldsymbol\\theta}-\\boldsymbol\\theta)^{\\mathsf T}\n\\widehat\\Omega^{-1}\n(\\widehat{\\boldsymbol\\theta}-\\boldsymbol\\theta)\n\\Rightarrow\\chi_7^2. \\tag{34}\n\\]\nTaking the \\((1-\\alpha_0)\\)-quantile in (34) proves\n\\[\nP(\\boldsymbol\\theta\\in\\mathcal C_\\theta)\\longrightarrow1-\\alpha_0.\n\\]",
